### What version of Hono are you using?

4.9.6

### What runtime/platform is your app running on? (with version if possible)

Deno (latest Supabase hosted version - I think 2.1 it is currently)

### What steps can reproduce the bug?

1. Create a new Supabase project and set it up
2. Create a new edge function using `supabase functions new test`
3. Disable JWT verifiction in the `config.toml` by setting `verify_jwt=false` for the newly created test function
4. Add the following in the `index.ts` file
5. Call this function with any form-data that includes a reasonably large file to introduce some network delay (for me this was 500KB given a 50MB/s upload rate)

```ts
import "jsr:@supabase/functions-js/edge-runtime.d.ts"
import { Hono } from "npm:hono"

const app = new Hono()

app.use("*", async (c, next) => {
  return c.json(401)
})

app.post("/test", async (c) => {
  const formData = await c.req.formData()
  return c.json(200)
})

Deno.serve(app.fetch)
```


### What is the expected behavior?

- It should return a `401`

### What do you see instead?

- This function will never return 
- You only see a boot and shutdown in the Supabase logs
- Testing it locally with `deno run` is only partially possible as there is no network delay (maybe with huge files)

#### Supabase Edge Function Logs
<img width="389" height="82" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/656a33d3-4f7c-4ff7-9727-695ee8bfed47" />

### Additional information

This function in contrast works seamlessly and returns a `200`

```ts
import "jsr:@supabase/functions-js/edge-runtime.d.ts"
import { Hono } from "npm:hono"

const app = new Hono()

// NOTE this commented out middleware
// app.use("*", async (c, next) => {
//   return c.json(401)
// })

app.post("/test", async (c) => {
  const formData = await c.req.formData()
  return c.json(200)
})

Deno.serve(app.fetch)
```

I suspect that this has something to do with the body being parsed while the middleware already wants to return a value. Probably even the body isn't fully received while the middleware already wants to return. 

I further noticed that if one adds the [Hono Body Limit Middleware](https://hono.dev/docs/middleware/builtin/body-limit) it is also completely ignored. Don't know if it ever worked or if it is just this example that breaks things (especially when using multiple middlewares?). 

**Bottom line is that there is something broken when using larger files (which introduce a network delay) and any Hono middleware.**

